43
00:03:43,406 --> 00:03:46,212
So, language modeling, this is a quick recap.

44
00:03:46,992 --> 00:03:53,444
Language models at a high level are simply models of probability distribution over sequences of tokens or of words.

45
00:03:53,774 --> 00:04:03,012
So it's basically some model of P of X1 to XL, where X1 is basically word one and XL is the last word in the sequence or in the sentence.

46
00:04:04,112 --> 00:04:16,272
so very concretely, if you have a sentence like the mouse ate the cheese, what the language model gives you is simply a probability of this sentence being uttered by a human or being found on on online.

47
00:04:17,072 --> 00:04:31,300
so if you have another sentence like the the mouse ate cheese, here there's grammatical mistakes, so the model should know that this should have some syntactic knowledge, so it should know that this has less likelihood of appearing online.

48
00:04:32,432 --> 00:04:41,444
if you have another sentence like the cheese ate the mouse, then the model should hopefully know about the fact that usually cheese don't eat mouse.

49
00:04:42,032 --> 00:04:45,124
so there's some semantic knowledge, and this is less likely than the first sentence.

50
00:04:45,454 --> 00:04:47,972
So this is basically at a high level what language models are.

51
00:04:49,998 --> 00:04:53,572
one word that you probably have been hearing a lot in the news are generative models.

52
00:04:54,112 --> 00:04:59,300
so this is just something that can generate models that can generate sentences or can generate some data.

53
00:04:59,632 --> 00:05:07,332
the reason why we say language models are generative models is that once you have a model of a distribution, you can simply sample from this model and then we can generate data.

54
00:05:07,872 --> 00:05:11,300
so you can generate sentences using a language model.

55
00:05:12,686 --> 00:05:18,468
So the type of models that people are all currently using are what we call autoregressive language models.

56
00:05:19,118 --> 00:05:35,392
And the key idea of auto-regressive language models is that you take this distribution over words, and you basically decompose it into the distribution of the first word, multiplied by the likelihood of the distribution of the second word.

57
00:05:35,566 --> 00:05:40,100
given the first word, multiply it by p, of the third word, given the first two words.

58
00:05:41,326 --> 00:05:42,832
so there's no approximation here.

59
00:05:42,912 --> 00:05:45,872
This is just a chain rule of probability, which hopefully you all know about.

60
00:05:46,272 --> 00:05:47,232
really no approximation.

61
00:05:47,312 --> 00:05:49,828
This is just one way of modeling a distribution.

62
00:05:50,592 --> 00:05:59,044
so slightly more concisely, you can write it as a product of p's of the next word given everything which happened in the past, so of the context.

63
00:05:59,712 --> 00:06:02,500
so this is what we call autoregressive language models.

64
00:06:02,752 --> 00:06:06,272
Again, this is really not the only way of modeling distribution.

65
00:06:06,352 --> 00:06:07,396
This is just one way.

66
00:06:07,952 --> 00:06:10,272
it has some benefits and some downsides.

67
00:06:10,512 --> 00:06:22,832
One downside of autoregressive language models is that when you actually sample from this autoregressive language model, you basically have a for loop which generates the next word, then conditions on that next word, and then regenerate the other word.

68
00:06:22,958 --> 00:06:27,812
So basically, if you have a longer sentence that you want to generate, you it takes more time to generate it.

69
00:06:28,272 --> 00:06:33,988
so there are some downsides of this current paradigm, but that's what we currently have, so I'm going to talk about this one.

70
00:06:35,872 --> 00:06:36,352
great.

71
00:06:36,814 --> 00:06:38,212
So autoregressive language models.

72
00:06:38,352 --> 00:06:44,032
At a high level, um, what the task of autoregressive language model is, is simply predicting the next word, as I just said.

73
00:06:44,334 --> 00:06:49,508
So if you have a sentence like she likely prefers, uh one potential next word might be dogs.

74
00:06:50,542 --> 00:06:54,116
And the what the way we do it is that we first tokenize.

75
00:06:54,574 --> 00:07:00,512
So you take these words or subwords, you tokenize them, and then you give an ID for each token.

76
00:07:00,592 --> 00:07:02,020
So here you have one, two, three.

77
00:07:03,072 --> 00:07:07,300
then you pass it through this black box, as I already said, we're not going to talk about the architecture.

78
00:07:07,406 --> 00:07:15,812
You just pass it through a model, and you then get a distribution, a probability distribution, over the next word over the next token.

79
00:07:16,814 --> 00:07:22,724
And then you sample from this distribution, you get a new token, and then you detokenize.

80
00:07:22,990 --> 00:07:27,844
So you get a new ID, you then detokenize, and that's how you basically sample from a language model.

81
00:07:28,352 --> 00:07:33,796
one thing which is important to note is that the last two steps, uh, two steps were actually only needed during inference.

82
00:07:34,352 --> 00:07:46,884
when you do training, you just need to predict the most likely token, and you can just compare to the real token, which happened in practice, and then you basically change the weights of your model to increase the probability of generating that token.

