00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:19,300
at a high level, it's basically just your validation loss.

213
00:19:20,174 --> 00:19:29,360
the slight difference with perplexity is that we use something that is slightly more interpretable, which is that we use the average per token loss, and then you exponentiate it.

214
00:19:29,518 --> 00:19:40,676
And the reason why you exponentiate it is because you want, I mean, the loss has a log inside, and you like one, humans are actually pretty bad at thinking in log space, but two, logs depend on the base of the log.

215
00:19:41,280 --> 00:19:47,620
while when you exponentiate, you basically have everything in the kind of the vocabulary size unit.

216
00:19:48,654 --> 00:19:53,476
and the average per token is just so that your perplexity is independent of the length of your sequence.

217
00:19:54,160 --> 00:19:58,692
so perplexity is just two to the power average of the loss of the sequence.

218
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:05,440
so perplexity is between one and the length of the vocabulary of your tokenizer.

219
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:14,052
one it's simply, well, if you predict perfectly the thing which every word, then every word will have basically product of ones.

